/-
# Products of nilpotent groups with the minimum condition (Kourovka Notebook Problem 2.69)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 2.69 (N. F. Sesekin): let a group `G` be the
product of two subgroups `A` and `B`, each of which is nilpotent and satisfies the minimum
condition. Prove or refute the following: (a) `G` is soluble; (b) the divisible parts of `A` and `B`
commute elementwise.
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Nilpotent
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Solvable
public import Mathlib.Order.CompletePartialOrder

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ProductNilpotentMinimalConditionKourovka2Problem69Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 2.69 (N. F.
Sesekin). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700139 (`KOU-2.69`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

`G = AB` means every element is a product `a b` with `a ∈ A`, `b ∈ B`; the minimum condition on
subgroups is `WellFoundedLT (Subgroup A)`; the divisible part of `A` is the subgroup generated by
all divisible subgroups of `A` (in a nilpotent group with the minimum condition this is its largest
divisible subgroup).
-/

/-- The divisible part of a subgroup `A`: the subgroup generated by all divisible subgroups
`S ≤ A` (every `s ∈ S` has an `n`-th root in `S` for every `n ≥ 1`). -/
def divisiblePart {G : Type} [Group G] (A : Subgroup G) : Subgroup G :=
  ⨆ (S : Subgroup G) (_ : S ≤ A) (_ : ∀ s ∈ S, ∀ n : ℕ, 0 < n → ∃ t ∈ S, t ^ n = s), S

/-- Kourovka 2.69(a) (N. F. Sesekin): if a group `G = AB` is the product of two nilpotent
subgroups `A`, `B` satisfying the minimum condition on subgroups, then `G` is soluble. -/
def productNilpotentMinimalConditionSoluble : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] (A B : Subgroup G), Group.IsNilpotent A → Group.IsNilpotent B →
    WellFoundedLT (Subgroup A) → WellFoundedLT (Subgroup B) →
    (∀ g : G, ∃ a ∈ A, ∃ b ∈ B, g = a * b) → Group.IsSolvable G

/-- Kourovka 2.69(b): under the same hypotheses, the divisible parts of `A` and `B` commute
elementwise. -/
def productNilpotentMinimalConditionDivisiblePartsCommute : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] (A B : Subgroup G), Group.IsNilpotent A → Group.IsNilpotent B →
    WellFoundedLT (Subgroup A) → WellFoundedLT (Subgroup B) →
    (∀ g : G, ∃ a ∈ A, ∃ b ∈ B, g = a * b) →
      ∀ x ∈ divisiblePart A, ∀ y ∈ divisiblePart B, x * y = y * x

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive records that both parts of 2.69 are proved (Chernikov, 1980);
this is part (a), solubility of `G`. Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved
Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of
Solved Problems, Problem 2.69 (N. F. Sesekin); resolution: N. S. Chernikov, Soviet Math. Dokl. 21
(1980), 701-703, Kourovka Archive, Problem 2.69: both parts are proved (Chernikov),
https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
public theorem_wanted productNilpotentMinimalConditionSoluble_holds :
    productNilpotentMinimalConditionSoluble

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive records that both parts of 2.69 are proved (Chernikov, 1980);
this is part (b), the divisible parts of `A` and `B` commute elementwise. Source: E. I. Khukhro and
V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue,
arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 2.69 (N. F. Sesekin); resolution: N.
S. Chernikov, Soviet Math. Dokl. 21 (1980), 701-703, Kourovka Archive, Problem 2.69: both parts are
proved (Chernikov), https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
public theorem_wanted productNilpotentMinimalConditionDivisiblePartsCommute_holds :
    productNilpotentMinimalConditionDivisiblePartsCommute

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ProductNilpotentMinimalConditionKourovka2Problem69Wanted
